Cervical dysplasia
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Asymptomatic presentation, High-risk HPV positivity, Acetowhite changes on colposcopy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-009
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of acute, severe pelvic anxiety and moderate, continuous vaginal bleeding following intercourse, requiring immediate evaluation to rule out acute laceration.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute onset of continuous, unexplained vaginal bleeding post-intercourse in a patient with a known history of high-risk HPV, accompanied by a severe panic attack regarding potential cancer rupture. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively identify the source of bleeding (rule out vault laceration vs. minor cervical trauma), arrest the hemorrhage, and provide profound acute anxiolysis to prevent clinical deterioration before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I had a positive HPV test last week, and tonight after sex I started bleeding constantly, I am terrified I have torn a tumor.
History of Present Illness: The patient was generally entirely asymptomatic until receiving a routine screening result indicating high-risk human papillomavirus (HPV) infection five days ago. Tonight, immediately following intercourse, she developed acute, continuous postcoital vaginal bleeding. She reports the bleeding is heavier than typical spotting, soaking two panty liners in the last hour. She denies deep pelvic pain but reports acute, overwhelming anxiety and hyperventilation. She reports a history of abnormal, persistent, thin vaginal discharge over the past month.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently impaired by acute severe panic and hyperventilation.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; acute psychological crisis compounding a physical symptom.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/82 mmHg | HR: 108 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 66 Kg
General: Patient is acutely distressed, hyperventilating, and pacing. No clinical pallor.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is soft and non-tender. Speculum examination reveals a grossly normal-appearing cervix on standard visual inspection without any large exophytic masses or deep ulcerations. A small, superficial 3mm mucosal abrasion is noted on the posterior cervical lip, oozing a small amount of dark blood. No vaginal vault lacerations identified. (Note: Acetowhite epithelial changes, punctation, or mosaicism typically visible only after application of 5% acetic acid during colposcopy are not assessed in this acute primary care setting).
Environmental Observations: Safe, clean environment.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Minor Cervical Trauma post-coitus (Background: Known High-Risk HPV/Suspected Cervical Dysplasia) - Status: Stable bleeding
Differentials:

Vaginal Vault Laceration - Ruled out by direct visual inspection confirming intact vaginal walls and fornices.

Invasive Cervical Carcinoma - Unlikely given the grossly normal-appearing cervix without friable exophytic masses or deep, necrotic ulcers on inspection.

Ectopic Pregnancy - Ruled out due to negative point-of-care urine pregnancy test and absence of deep pelvic pain or adnexal tenderness.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with continuous postcoital bleeding and a severe acute stress reaction, driven by a recent high-risk HPV diagnosis. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically mandated to swiftly differentiate between a benign superficial abrasion and a severe vaginal laceration or actively hemorrhaging occult mass. Objective speculum examination successfully identified a minor, benign cervical abrasion as the source, definitively ruling out immediate life-threatening hemorrhage. The provision of local hemostasis, combined with immediate clinical reassurance and de-escalation of the severe panic attack, could not have been delayed until the next in-hours period without resulting in an unnecessary, highly distressed emergency department presentation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Applied direct pressure to the superficial cervical abrasion with a large swab via speculum for 3 minutes, resulting in complete cessation of oozing. Provided acute psychological de-escalation and reassurance regarding the grossly normal appearance of the cervix.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage acute panic, utilize a paper bag or cupped hands to rebreathe air when hyperventilating; feeling the respiratory rate slow down will reinforce independent control over panic symptoms. To allow the minor abrasion to heal, maintain strict pelvic rest (no intercourse, tampons, or douching) for the next 7 days; observing the total absence of further spotting will validate adherence to this resting period.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 7 days to discuss the recent HPV result and arrange formal colposcopy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Attend the local Emergency Department if the vaginal bleeding restarts and becomes heavy enough to soak through a standard menstrual pad in less than an hour, or if you develop severe, sharp lower abdominal pain.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The cervix appears grossly normal to the naked eye, but formal colposcopy remains essential to fully evaluate the HPV result.
Clinical Course: The minor bleeding has been completely stopped. Dysplasia progresses very slowly over years, not days; an acute cancer rupture did not occur tonight.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if bleeding heavily resumes, or if you develop severe, deep pelvic pain.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The recent positive HPV test simply means the virus is present, which can slowly cause cell changes (dysplasia) over many years, but it is not currently a visible cancer. The bleeding tonight was merely from a tiny, superficial scrape on the cervix during intercourse, which we have stopped with gentle pressure.
Actionable Steps: Avoid inserting anything into the vagina for a full week to allow the scrape to heal completely, and ensure you attend your GP appointment to schedule the required microscopic examination (colposcopy).

